  <h2>Age and Temperature Sensitivity</h2>
  <p>
    Temperature sensitivity during sleep changes significantly with age, and this has implications for how different members of the same household may need to configure their sleep environment.
  </p>
  <p>
    Young adults (20–40) generally have robust thermoregulatory capacity and can achieve good deep sleep across a moderate temperature range (16–22°C) as long as humidity is controlled. Their peripheral vasodilation response — the warming of hands and feet that signals sleep preparation — is efficient, and they tolerate moderate deviations from optimal temperature with less impact on sleep architecture.
  </p>
  <p>
    Middle-aged adults (40–60) show the beginning of thermoregulatory decline. The efficiency of peripheral vasodilation decreases, meaning the core temperature drop needed for deep sleep onset takes longer and is less complete. For women approaching or in menopause, vasomotor symptoms (hot flashes) can profoundly disrupt sleep even in an otherwise optimally temperature-controlled room — a problem that requires a different set of interventions than simple room temperature management.
  </p>
  <p>
    Older adults (65+) have substantially reduced thermoregulatory capacity. The peripheral vasodilation response is weaker, baseline body temperature tends to be slightly lower, and the temperature range within which they can effectively thermoregulate during sleep is narrower. Elderly sleepers are more sensitive to both cold (which can suppress REM) and heat (which fragments N3). A slightly warmer optimal range — 19–21°C rather than 17–19°C — is appropriate for most older adults, particularly in climates with cold winters.
  </p>
  <p>
    For families caring for elderly parents — a context central to SleepTech's heritage and to the multi-generational households common across Japan and Southeast Asia — understanding these age-related differences in temperature sensitivity is important for configuring the monitoring and climate environment appropriately for each family member.
  </p>

  <h2>What YumePad Sleep Data Reveals About Temperature Effects</h2>
  <p>
    Because the YumePad system captures sleep stage data alongside ambient temperature and humidity readings (logged either manually in the context log or automatically through smart home integration), it is possible to build individual-level correlational evidence about how temperature affects each user's sleep architecture specifically.
  </p>
  <p>
    In aggregate user data, the YumePad population-level analysis shows clear thresholds: N3 duration begins to decline measurably when bedroom temperature exceeds 23°C, with a steeper decline above 25°C. The effect is approximately linear in the 20–28°C range. Overnight HRV shows a similar temperature sensitivity, declining in step with N3 duration as temperature rises.
  </p>
  <p>
    At the individual level, the variation is substantial. Some users show minimal sleep architecture change up to 26°C; others show significant N3 suppression above 21°C. Personal temperature sensitivity — determined from the user's own longitudinal data — is now surfaced in the YumePad weekly report as a specific metric, allowing users to identify their personal thermal threshold rather than relying on population averages.
  </p>

