  <h2>The Smart Bed: Closing the Loop</h2>
  <p>
    Manual temperature management — adjusting an air conditioner or thermostat before bed — is a fixed-point solution to a dynamic problem. The body's thermal needs during sleep are not static across the night. They shift with sleep stage: cooler during N3, tolerating slightly warmer during REM, needing responsive adjustment as the body moves through its ultradian 90-minute cycles.
  </p>
  <p>
    SleepTech's forthcoming Smart Bed product is designed to close this loop. Using real-time sleep stage data from the YumePad sensor integrated into the mattress surface, the Smart Bed's climate control layer — a thin active-temperature mesh embedded in the mattress — adjusts surface temperature in real time to match the body's stage-specific thermal requirements. During N3 entry and maintenance, the surface cools to support the core temperature drop needed for deep sleep. As the body transitions to REM, the surface temperature rises slightly to prevent the overcooling that disrupts late-night REM architecture.
  </p>
  <p>
    In pilot testing with 200 participants across Tokyo and Singapore, users sleeping on the Smart Bed prototype showed a 17% increase in N3 duration and a 12% improvement in overnight HRV compared to the same participants sleeping in the same room with conventional bedding and standard air conditioning at a fixed temperature. The improvement was largest in the 45–65 age group, consistent with the age-related thermoregulatory decline described above.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Smart Bed represents the first time that real-time sleep stage data and active climate control have been integrated into a single consumer product — a convergence that has been technically feasible for several years but required the sleep staging accuracy of a mature BCG-based sensor system to be clinically meaningful. SleepTech's 2012 founding in Osaka gave the company over a decade to develop and validate that sensor platform before applying it to the climate control problem.
  </p>

